WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
Over 75,000 ferrets released to control rabbits instead reduced native ground nesting birds.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.900
This is unintended consequences, episode 178.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.520
In 1881, New Zealand's Rabbit Newsants Act gave ferrets official protection as natural

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:19.220
enemies of the rabbit and empowered inspectors to enforce control on landowners.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.600
Pastoral runs in the southern South Island had already seen wool clip and laming rates

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.960
fall sharply from rabbit damage by the mid-1870s.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.700
The act was designed to cut rabbit numbers on grazing land.

00:00:32.787 --> 00:00:37.287
Pastoralist Cuthbert Cowan brought the first documented private shipment of ferrets to

00:00:37.287 --> 00:00:43.127
his Southland property in 1876, after rabbits had begun to reduce sheep numbers on his runs.

00:00:43.787 --> 00:00:49.027
Rabbits had arrived with whalers and early settlers from the 1820s through the 1850s,

00:00:49.347 --> 00:00:51.987
and multiplied once pastoral farming expanded.

00:00:52.587 --> 00:00:57.847
By the early 1870s, burrowing and pasture loss were cutting wool production and land values

00:00:57.847 --> 00:01:03.087
across Otago and Southland. Government rental income from affected runs dropped from

00:01:03.087 --> 00:01:11.807
2,288 pounds to 619 pounds by 1881. Superintending rabbit inspector Benjamin P. Bailey and local

00:01:11.807 --> 00:01:16.407
runholders turned to ferrets as a cheaper alternative to repeated poisoning and trapping.

00:01:17.047 --> 00:01:21.387
The axe shielded mustalids from harm and directed inspectors to promote their use.

00:01:22.007 --> 00:01:25.207
The choice rested on ferrets killing rabbits efficiently in Britain,

00:01:25.207 --> 00:01:29.287
and on the urgent need to protect the colony's dominant export income.

00:01:29.887 --> 00:01:34.367
Cowan had already shown on his own land that ferrets could be bred locally and released

00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:39.327
without waiting for slow, expensive imports from England, which often arrived weakened

00:01:39.327 --> 00:01:40.427
after months at sea.

00:01:41.147 --> 00:01:42.607
The arithmetic looked straightforward.

00:01:43.307 --> 00:01:47.607
Each ferret could in principle remove dozens of rabbits a season and spare the recurring

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:51.247
labor of trappers covering thousands of hectares of open tussic.

00:01:51.247 --> 00:01:56.887
Fines of ten pounds on anyone who killed a protected mustalade gave landowners a legal

00:01:56.887 --> 00:02:01.087
reason to tolerate the new arrivals rather than treat the ferrets as another pest.

00:02:01.747 --> 00:02:05.987
Officials viewed the program as an extension of familiar British game-keeping practice.

00:02:06.971 --> 00:02:12.871
The Department of Agriculture formed in 1892, placed rabbit control under its own inspectors

00:02:12.871 --> 00:02:16.351
and spent roughly a quarter of its 1895 budget on the work.

00:02:17.031 --> 00:02:22.871
Government contracts purchased 21,760 ferrets in 1887 to 88 alone.

00:02:23.571 --> 00:02:27.371
Otago stations recorded more than 7,000 releases in 1890.

00:02:28.031 --> 00:02:34.211
The Amori Rabbit Board liberated 1,000 ferrets plus stoats, weasels and cats in 1888 to 89.

00:02:34.931 --> 00:02:39.491
Private breeding stations supplied additional animals, ed up to 10 shillings each for release

00:02:39.491 --> 00:02:41.111
on crown pastoral leases.

00:02:41.111 --> 00:02:47.251
Historian R. M. Burden later estimated the liberations running from 1870 to 1920.

00:02:47.931 --> 00:02:52.671
Early reports noted local drops and rabbit numbers on some runs, which encouraged further

00:02:52.671 --> 00:02:57.471
shipments even as stoats and weasels were added in at least 25 separate consignments between

00:02:57.471 --> 00:02:59.491
1883 and 1892.

00:03:00.351 --> 00:03:04.471
Inspectors tracked the numbers released per station and recorded survival rates on the

00:03:04.471 --> 00:03:08.191
open plains, where cover was scarce compared with English hedgerows.

00:03:08.191 --> 00:03:12.751
The inspectors responded by increasing the scale of releases rather than questioning

00:03:12.751 --> 00:03:13.391
the method.

00:03:13.751 --> 00:03:18.891
When initial ferrets stocks proved insufficient, local breeding colonies on several South Island

00:03:18.891 --> 00:03:23.711
runs produced young animals on site for release the same season, cutting both transport

00:03:23.711 --> 00:03:24.711
losses and costs.

00:03:25.431 --> 00:03:29.731
The same records show that stots and weasels were added once ferret numbers alone appeared

00:03:29.731 --> 00:03:33.051
inadequate to match rabbit breeding on the vast leasehold blocks.

00:03:33.051 --> 00:03:38.782
Fairots proved less specialized than hoped, and readily took ground-nesting birds whose populations

00:03:38.782 --> 00:03:41.102
were already thinned by ship rats and cats.

00:03:41.762 --> 00:03:47.002
University of Waikato researcher Carolyn King's review of 1880's newspapers and committee

00:03:47.002 --> 00:03:52.482
evidence shows that Mustalids preyed on Kiwi, Weka, and Blue Duck chicks as readily as

00:03:52.482 --> 00:03:52.962
on rabbits.

00:03:53.622 --> 00:04:00.222
The same 1884 joint committee testimony that recorded Cowan's 26,000 rabbit kill also documented

00:04:00.222 --> 00:04:03.522
continued pasture damage once rabbit numbers rebounded.

00:04:03.862 --> 00:04:09.002
Because ferrets could not keep pace with rabbit breeding rates on open tussic country, landowners

00:04:09.002 --> 00:04:13.662
continued to press for more releases while native bird populations declined without similar

00:04:13.662 --> 00:04:14.442
public notice.

00:04:15.082 --> 00:04:21.542
At least 7,838 stots and weasels arrived in the same period, adding further predation pressure.

00:04:22.062 --> 00:04:27.662
By 1900, the combined mustalid population was established across the South Island, contributing

00:04:27.662 --> 00:04:29.782
leading to the mainland extinction of Cacapal.

00:04:30.442 --> 00:04:35.602
Rabbit boards formed after 1947 still inherited a landscape where the original biological

00:04:35.602 --> 00:04:40.602
control program had failed to prevent either the target pest or the non-target losses.

00:04:41.322 --> 00:04:46.622
On the open canterbury and Otago plains, rabbits could retreat into burrows or disperse

00:04:46.622 --> 00:04:51.602
across hundreds of hectares, while ground nesting birds had no such refuge once a ferret

00:04:51.602 --> 00:04:52.482
located a nest.

00:04:52.482 --> 00:04:59.042
The 1881 Axe Protection Clause prevented any systematic removal of the mustelads even

00:04:59.042 --> 00:05:02.422
after early signs of bird losses appeared in station diaries.

00:05:02.942 --> 00:05:08.022
Because the inspector's performance was measured by rabbit counts on pastoral leases rather

00:05:08.022 --> 00:05:13.202
than by counts of surviving native species, the broader ecological cost remained outside

00:05:13.202 --> 00:05:14.262
the formal accounting.

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:20.650
Official government purchases of ferrets for release ended in 1889, though rabbit boards

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:24.330
and private landowners continued liberations into the 20th century.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:29.670
Later legislation shifted emphasis to organized poisoning and, after 1989,

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:34.010
to regional councils that inherited both rabbit and predator problems.

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:38.930
Ferrets remain widespread and continue to limit recovery of several endemic birds.

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:43.070
The Department of Conservation lists ferrets among the introduced predators

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:46.850
whose presence compounds the effects of earlier rat and cat introductions.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:52.210
No subsequent program has fully reversed the combined impacts on ground-nesting species.

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:57.270
The shift to poisoning reduced some rabbit pressure, but left the established mustelid

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:58.610
populations untouched.

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:03.910
So the secondary predation on birds persisted, even as rabbit boards were later disbanded.

00:06:04.648 --> 00:06:10.168
When Benjamin Bailey's inspectors protected and released ferrets under the 1881 Act, the

00:06:10.168 --> 00:06:14.788
rule rewarded any animal that killed rabbits on pastoral leases without measuring effects

00:06:14.788 --> 00:06:15.708
on other species.

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:21.608
Later, Rabbit Board funding formulas continued to reward rapid visible kills over long-term

00:06:21.608 --> 00:06:22.808
ecosystem accounting.

00:06:23.248 --> 00:06:27.988
A control agent can look cheap and familiar, borrowed from a practice that already works

00:06:27.988 --> 00:06:32.388
somewhere else, and still be scored only on the pest the budget was written to shrink.

00:06:32.968 --> 00:06:37.428
Diet and range then sit outside the ledger until the losses are already widespread.

00:06:38.048 --> 00:06:42.868
Decision makers today can test whether a proposed control agent's diet and habitat range

00:06:42.868 --> 00:06:46.568
have been measured against all resident species before a large-scale release.

00:06:47.168 --> 00:06:52.288
The same test applies when modern agencies consider a new biological agent for any single

00:06:52.288 --> 00:06:52.688
pest.

00:06:53.368 --> 00:06:58.308
Environmental intelligence follows how those measurements get built, or skipped when a control

00:06:58.308 --> 00:07:00.948
plan moves from a trial plot to a whole region.

00:07:01.708 --> 00:07:06.768
If an agency near you is about to release something to suppress one damaging species, what

00:07:06.768 --> 00:07:08.408
would you watch for in the scorecard?

00:07:08.408 --> 00:07:13.228
only the pest counts or the species that share the ground and have nowhere else to go.

00:07:13.848 --> 00:07:18.568
And that's where today's story leaves us. If it changed how you see one everyday decision,

00:07:18.868 --> 00:07:24.548
that's the whole point. Pass it along to someone who depreciate it, and we'll be back tomorrow with another case.

